                    <h2 id="what-is">What Is Credit Utilization?</h2>
                    <p>Credit utilization is the percentage of your available revolving credit that you are currently using. According to the <a href="https://www.consumerfinance.gov/ask-cfpb/what-is-a-credit-report-and-credit-score-en-207/" class="underline">CFPB</a>, it's calculated per card and across all cards. Formula is simple:</p>
                    <div class="mt-4 rounded-xl bg-slate-900 text-white p-5 font-mono text-[14px]">Utilization % = (Current Statement Balance ÷ Total Credit Limit) × 100</div>
                    <p>There are two versions lenders see: <b>per-card utilization</b> and <b>overall utilization</b>. Both matter. If you have one card with a $300 limit and you owe $150, that's 50% utilization — even if your other cards are at 0%.</p>
                    <ul>
                        <li><b>Revolving accounts only:</b> Credit cards and lines of credit count. Installment loans like auto or student loans don't.</li>
                        <li><b>Reported monthly:</b> Your issuer typically reports your balance on your statement closing date, not your payment due date.</li>
                        <li><b>No memory in newer models:</b> Per Experian and Equifax, utilization has no long-term memory in FICO 10 and VantageScore 4.0 — fix it this month, and your score can recover next month.</li>
                    </ul>

                    <h2 id="why-30">Why Does 30% Matter? It's 30% of Your FICO Score</h2>
                    <p>There are five FICO components. Amounts owed is 30% of your score — the second biggest after payment history (35%). According to <a href="https://www.experian.com/blogs/ask-experian/credit-utilization-ratio/" class="underline">Experian</a> and <a href="https://www.equifax.com/personal/education/credit-score/articles/what-is-credit-utilization/" class="underline">Equifax</a>, low utilization signals you aren't overextended.</p>
                    <div class="mt-4 grid md:grid-cols-2 gap-4 not-prose">
                        <div class="rounded-xl border p-4 bg-white">
                            <div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest text-slate-400">FICO FACTORS</div><ul class="mt-2 space-y-1 text-[13px] leading-6">
                            <li>35% Payment History</li>
                            <li><b class="text-emerald-600">30% Amounts Owed (Utilization)</b></li>
                            <li>15% Length of History</li>
                            <li>10% Credit Mix</li>
                            <li>10% New Credit</li>
                        </ul>
                    </div>
                    <div class="rounded-xl border p-4 bg-emerald-50 border-emerald-100"><div class="text-[13px] font-bold">What lenders think over 30% means:</div><p class="mt-2 text-[13px] leading-5 text-slate-600">You rely heavily on credit to cover expenses. Risk models associate higher utilization with higher likelihood of missing payments within 12 months, even if you pay in full.</p></div>
                </div>
                <p>The commonly taught <b>Keep Under 30% Rule</b> is a beginner guardrail, not a law. It comes from CFPB financial coaching: keeping utilization under 30% keeps you out of the high-risk band in most scoring models used in the US.</p>

                <h2 id="ideal-10">Ideal Utilization: Why Under 10% is Best</h2>
                <p>If 30% keeps you safe, under 10% puts you in the excellent tier. Data from Experian shows consumers with FICO 780+ average around 5-7% utilization.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>0% isn't perfect:</b> Showing a tiny balance like $5-$20 can be better than $0 across all cards, because active use reports positive activity. But 0% overall won't hurt you.</li>
                    <li><b>1-9% is sweet spot:</b> For US beginners rebuilding with secured cards, aim for $3-$27 on a $300 card.</li>
                    <li><b>10-29% is good:</b> Still considered responsible, especially if you're new-to-credit with short history.</li>
                    <li><b>30%+ is where you start losing points fast:</b> 50%, 75%, 100% utilization can drop scores by 30-60 points quickly.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="examples">Real Examples With a $300 Limit Card</h2>
                <p>Most ScoreUp readers start with a $200-$300 secured card like Chime Secured or Discover it Secured. Here's what the math looks like on $300:</p>
                <div class="mt-4 overflow-x-auto not-prose">
                    <table class="w-full text-[13px] border rounded-xl overflow-hidden">
                        <thead class="bg-slate-900 text-white text-left"><tr><th class="p-3">Balance Reported</th><th class="p-3">Utilization</th><th class="p-3">Rating (US)</th></tr></thead>
                        <tbody class="bg-white">
                            <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3">$0</td><td class="p-3">0%</td><td class="p-3">Good (inactive)</td></tr>
                            <tr class="border-t bg-emerald-50/50"><td class="p-3">$9</td><td class="p-3">3%</td><td class="p-3 font-bold text-emerald-700">Excellent — ideal</td></tr>
                            <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3">$27</td><td class="p-3">9%</td><td class="p-3">Excellent</td></tr>
                            <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3">$60</td><td class="p-3">20%</td><td class="p-3">Good</td></tr>
                            <tr class="border-t"><td class="p-3">$90</td><td class="p-3">30%</td><td class="p-3">Limit — stay under this</td></tr>
                            <tr class="border-t bg-amber-50/70"><td class="p-3">$150</td><td class="p-3">50%</td><td class="p-3 text-amber-800">High — score impact</td></tr>
                            <tr class="border-t bg-red-50"><td class="p-3">$300</td><td class="p-3">100%</td><td class="p-3 text-red-700">Maxed — avoid</td></tr>
                        </tbody>
                    </table>
                </div>
                <h3>Overall utilization example</h3>
                <p>If you have Card A: $300 limit / $60 balance (20%) and Card B: $700 limit / $0 balance (0%). Total credit = $1000, total balance = $60. Overall utilization = 6% — excellent, even though Card A alone is 20%.</p>
                <p>That's why beginners should keep both individual card and total utilization low.</p>

                <h2 id="manage">How to Manage Credit Utilization Like a Pro</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><b>Keep reporting balance low, not spending low:</b> You can spend $250 on a $300 card, just pay it down before your statement closes.</li>
                    <li><b>Set balance alerts:</b> At $60 on a $300 card (20%) and $90 (30%). Most US issuers offer this in app.</li>
                    <li><b>Ask for credit limit increase after 6 months:</b> Same $90 balance on $600 limit = 15% instead of 30%. Don't increase spending.</li>
                    <li><b>Add a second card to increase total limit:</b> But only if you can manage on-time payments. Opening new credit temporarily dings new credit factor.</li>
                    <li><b>Make two payments per month:</b> Split your bill — one mid-cycle, one before due date — keeps reported balance low.</li>
                    <li><b>Never close your oldest card:</b> Closing reduces total limit and increases utilization plus hurts length of history.</li>
                    <li><b>Authorize to report $0? Don't:</b> Some fintech cards let you choose not to report. You want reporting to build history.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="pay-before">The Best Trick: Pay Before Your Statement Closing Date</h2>
                <p>This is the trick former CFPB counselors teach beginners in the US. Your issuer reports what you owe on your statement closing date — usually 3-5 days before the payment due date. If you pay in full after the statement closes but before the due date, you still show high utilization that month.</p>
                <div class="mt-4 rounded-2xl border border-slate-200 bg-white p-5 not-prose">
                    <div class="text-[13px] font-bold">How to do it:</div>
                    <ol class="mt-2 list-decimal pl-5 space-y-2 text-[13px] leading-6 text-slate-600">
                        <li>Find your statement closing date in your app (e.g., 16th of each month) — different from due date (e.g., 13th next month).</li>
                        <li>Pay 90% of your balance 2 days BEFORE closing. Leave $5-$20 to report.</li>
                        <li>Let statement close with low utilization. Then pay remaining before due date to avoid interest.</li>
                        <li>Result: Low utilization reported to Experian, Equifax, and TransUnion, no interest.</li>
                    </ol>
                    <div class="mt-3 text-[11px] text-slate-500">Example: Spend $200 on $300 card → On 14th pay $190 → Statement on 16th reports $10 (3% util) → Pay $10 by due date. No SSN needed, no extra apps.</div>
                </div>
                <p>If you need your score high for an apartment or auto loan check next week, paying down to 1-3% 48 hours before the closing date is the fastest points boost — because utilization has no memory, lenders will see the new low number within one reporting cycle.</p>

                <div class="mt-8 rounded-2xl bg-slate-900 text-white p-6 not-prose">
                    <div class="text-[12px] font-bold tracking-widest text-emerald-400">NO SSN NEEDED • US FOCUS</div>
                    <div class="mt-2 text-[18px] font-bold leading-tight">ScoreUp tools never ask for your Social Security Number.</div>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-[13px] text-slate-400 leading-6">Check utilization with our free calculator and simulator. We use self-reported balances. No credit pull. Your SSN is not required for educational estimates. For official FICO, use AnnualCreditReport.com — the only official US source.</p>
